a4_pointcloud_registration,A4 — 点云配准与非刚性形状匹配 (Point Cloud Registration & Non-Rigid Shape Matching),spectral methods shape matching graph alignment CVPR ICCV 2024 2025,1,[PDF] Distilling Spectral Graph for Object-Context Aware Open-Vocabulary ...,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2025/papers/Kim_Distilling_Spectral_Graph_for_Object-Context_Aware_Open-Vocabulary_Semantic_Segmentation_CVPR_2025_paper.pdf,openaccess.thecvf.com,0.9997546,"3.2.1. Complementary Spectral Graph Matching We match graphs with contrasting structures, as shown in Fig 4, enabling VFM to supplement the object-level con-textual knowledge that CLIP alone struggles to capture. To match an optimal head-wise attention graph, we exploit the spectral field of both AVFM and ACLIP. Our graph-matching mechanism has two main steps as illustrated in Fig. 2(a): (I) examining the eigenvalues of each graph and (II) matching optimal graph pairs using the spectral distribution. [...] In CVPR, 2024. 6  Holger Caesar, Jasper Uijlings, and Vittorio Ferrari. COCO-Stuff: Thing and Stuff Classes in Context. In CVPR, 2018. [...] (II) Graph Matching via Spectral Distribution. After ob-taining eigenvalues for each head, we compute spectral dis-tances to quantify structural differences, creating a cost ma-trix C ∈Rh×h for each graph pair from VFM and CLIP: \ m a t hc al { C}_{ i j} = 1 - \m a t hca l { D }_W(\bar {\lambda }_\text {VFM}^i, \bar {\lambda }_\text {CLIP}^j), \quad \forall \, i, j \in \{1, \dots , h\} (5) where ¯ λi VFM and ¯ λj CLIP represent the normalized eigenval-ues of the i-th head of VFM and the j-th head of CLIP, respectively, and DW denotes the Wasserstein distance.",True
