a3_deep_matching,A3 — 深度匹配: 端到端对应学习 (Deep Feature Matching & End-to-End Correspondence),cost volume correlation layer optical flow learning theory 2024 2025,1,[PDF] Global Matching for Optical Flow with Confidence-Guided Refinement,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.28759,arxiv.org,0.99957937,"These design choices now form the structural blueprint for modern deep learn-ing architectures. The shift to end-to-end deep models started with FlowNet , introducing a correlation layer used to compute a cost volume, followed by FlowNet 2 , which added multiple refinement stages making use of warp-ing. Similarly, another pillar of traditional methods, i.e., coarse-to-fine spatial pyramid, has been adopted by SpyNet , while PWC-Net  combined spa-tial pyramid with warping and cost volumes. Further advancements still built on these three pillars to optimize volumetric efficiency , achieve lightweight footprints [19–21], while also progressively improving the training strategies to attain optimal performance from existing architectures . [...] Masked cost volume autoencoding for pretraining optical flow estimation. In: CVPR (2023) 59. Smith, L.N., Topin, N.: Super-convergence: Very fast training of neural networks using large learning rates. In: Artificial intelligence and machine learning for multi-domain operations applications. vol. 11006, pp. 369–386. SPIE (2019) 60. Sui, X., Li, S., Geng, X., Wu, Y., Xu, X., Liu, Y., Goh, R., Zhu, H.: CRAFT: Cross-attentional flow transformer for robust optical flow. In: CVPR (2022) 61. Sun, D., Herrmann, C., Reda, F., Rubinstein, M., Fleet, D.J., Freeman, W.T.: Disentangling architecture and training for optical flow. In: ECCV (2022) 62. Sun, D., Vlasic, D., Herrmann, C., Jampani, V., Krainin, M., Chang, H., Zabih, R., Freeman, W.T., Liu, C.: AutoFlow: Learning a better training set for [...] IEEE (2020) 70. Wang, Y., Deng, J.: WAFT: Warping-alone field transforms for optical flow. arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.21526 (2025) 71. Wang, Y., Lipson, L., Deng, J.: SEA-RAFT: Simple, efficient, accurate RAFT for optical flow. In: ECCV. Springer (2024) 72. Weinzaepfel, P., Lucas, T., Leroy, V., Cabon, Y., Arora, V., Brégier, R., Csurka, G., Antsfeld, L., Chidlovskii, B., Revaud, J.: CroCo v2: Improved cross-view com-pletion pre-training for stereo matching and optical flow. In: ICCV (2023) 73. Wen, H., Liang, E., Deng, J.: Layeredflow: A real-world benchmark for non-lambertian multi-layer optical flow. In: ECCV. pp. 477–495. Springer (2024) 74. Xu, H., Zhang, J., Cai, J., Rezatofighi, H., Tao, D.: GMFlow: Learning optical flow via global matching. In: CVPR (2022) 75. Xu, H., Zhang, J.,",True
a4_pointcloud_registration,A4 — 点云配准与非刚性形状匹配 (Point Cloud Registration & Non-Rigid Shape Matching),point cloud registration deep learning theory NeurIPS ICML 2024 2025,2,Registration of 3D Point Sets Using Exponential-based Similarity Matrix,https://arxiv.org/html/2505.04540v1,arxiv.org,0.9995523,"Despite the variety of available registration algorithms, point cloud alignment can still fail, particularly under conditions of large rotational transformations between source and target point sets. In light of this challenge, we propose a novel variant of the ICP algorithm that performs weighted matching using our proposed similarity matrix. We compare our method to both classical numerical approaches and deep learning-based techniques. Through experimental results, we demonstrate how our approach can effectively resolve these registration challenges. [...] As shown in Fig. 5, ESM-ICP outperforms all evaluated deep learning models under a wide range of transformation conditions. Quantitative results are presented in Table I for the Airplane class and in Table II for the Person class. Both illustrate the superior accuracy of ESM-ICP.

In contrast to deep learning models, Go-ICP  shows comparatively better performance under large rotations, however, it too can fail in scenarios with complex and coupled axis rotations. Moreover, Go-ICP , like most methods, struggles when the source point cloud is contaminated by noise. [...] We benchmark the performance of ESM-ICP against several baseline and state-of-the-art registration algorithms, including traditional geometry-based methods such as standard ICP [1, 3], ICP Point-to-Plane , Go-ICP , and ICP Non-Linear [40, 42], as well as learning-based methods like Deep Closest Point (DCP) , PointNetLK , RpmNet , and DeepGMR . To assess robustness, we also introduce outliers into the Source point cloud and analyze the algorithm’s performance under such perturbations.",True
a4_pointcloud_registration,A4 — 点云配准与非刚性形状匹配 (Point Cloud Registration & Non-Rigid Shape Matching),spectral methods shape matching graph alignment CVPR ICCV 2024 2025,2,[PDF] EchoMatch: Partial-to-Partial Shape Matching via Correspondence ...,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2025/papers/Xie_EchoMatch_Partial-to-Partial_Shape_Matching_via_Correspondence_Reflection_CVPR_2025_paper.pdf,openaccess.thecvf.com,0.9993687,"This is often dubbed non-rigid Iterative Closest Point (ICP) and typically is computationally costly. Recent efforts try to tackle deformation and matching tasks jointly with op-timality guarantees. However, the size of tractable prob-lems is limited: only either a handful of sparse key points or low-resolution shapes are matched [29, 53, 54, 63, 64]. On the other hand, spectral methods (e.g. functional maps) pio-neered by Ovsjanikov et al.  do not depend on the shape resolution but rather the number of spectral bases, which are typically the 50 ⇠200 leading eigen-bases of the Laplace-Beltrami-Operator (LBO) of the given shapes. Moreover, the resulting optimisation becomes a least squares prob-lem, which can be efﬁciently solved to optimality. Many learning-based methods built on this [...] Full-to-Full Shape Matching. Non-rigid shape match-ing methods can be categorised into two groups, namely spatial and spectral methods, depending on their operating domain. In the family of spatial methods, the most com-mon strategy is to deform the source to resemble the target shape and the correspondences are retrieved simply by near-est neighbour search in the embedding space [25, 26, 59]. [...] In CVPR, 2024. 2  Paul Roetzer, Paul Swoboda, Daniel Cremers, and Florian Bernard.
A scalable combinatorial solver for elastic geo-metrically consistent 3d shape matching. In CVPR, pages 428–438, 2022. 2, 3, 6, 7  Yusuf Sahillio˘ glu.",True
