First Eigenvector Second Eigenvector Spectral Traversal Scan Graph (V,E) A B C D Image Patches Spectral Decomposition Figure 2. The Spectral Traversal Scan (STS) architecture begins by representing image patches as a graph. The Laplacian spectrum of this graph is then generated, and its eigenvectors are computed. The traversal path for the image patches is determined by the order of eigenvectors. The 1st eigenvector traverses the foreground/background regions, the 2nd eigenvector focuses on the next most salient structures, and each subsequent eigenvector progressively captures finer details in the image. [...] 3.4. Spectral Traversal Scan In this section, we describe how the graph Laplacian eigen-spectrum is leveraged to define the travesal order of patches.
We begin by representing the image as a graph G = (V, E), where each node vi ∈V corresponds to a patch xi 23885 of the image, and edges eij ∈E connect pairs of nodes based on their spatial proximity. Following methods for spectral clustering  and normalized cuts , we con-sider a weighted adjacency matrix W of the graph, which is constructed using Euclidean distances between patches. [...] Spectral Graph Analysis studies the properties of a graph by analyzing the eigen-spectrum of its Laplacian matrix, which can be seen as a discrete version of the continuous Laplace-Beltrami operator obtained by a finite difference method . Given a real-valued function f ∈C2 on a Riemannian manifold M, the Laplace-Beltrami operator ∆ of f is defined as ∆f = div(grad f) where with grad f is the gradient of f and div denotes the divergence.",True
a3_deep_matching,A3 — 深度匹配: 端到端对应学习 (Deep Feature Matching & End-to-End Correspondence),graph neural network matching geometric learning NeurIPS ICML 2024 2025,3,NeurIPS Poster Bridging Equivariant GNNs and Spherical CNNs for Structured Physical Domains,https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/115117,neurips.cc,0.9791955,"Many modeling tasks from disparate domains can be framed the same way, computing spherical signals from geometric inputs, for example, computing the radar response of different objects or navigating through an environment. This paper introduces G2Sphere, a general method for mapping object geometries to spherical signals. G2Sphere operates entirely in Fourier space, encoding geometric structure into latent Fourier features using equivariant neural networks and outputting the Fourier coefficients of the continuous target signal, which can be evaluated at any resolution. By utilizing a hybrid GNN-spherical CNN architecture, our method achieves much higher frequency output signal than comparable equivariant GNNs and avoids hand-engineered geometry features used previously by purely spherical [...] geometry features used previously by purely spherical methods. We perform experiments on various challenging domains including radar response modeling, aerodynamic drag prediction, and policy learning for manipulation and navigation. We find that G2Sphere outperforms competitive baselines in terms of accuracy and inference time, and we demonstrate that equivariance and Fourier features lead to improved sample efficiency and generalization.The source code is available at: [...] Skip to yearly menu bar

