a1_keypoint_matching,A1 — 稀疏对应: 关键点匹配与特征对齐 (Sparse Correspondence: Keypoint Matching),feature matching correspondence field learning mathematical model 2024 2025,5,"R3PM-Net: Real-time, Robust, Real-world Point Matching Network",https://arxiv.org/html/2604.05060v2,arxiv.org,0.17634544,"### 3.2 Correspondence Estimation

Following the feature extraction step, the network predicts soft correspondences for the source and target point clouds. Instead of a binary assignment, a matching matrix M∈[0,1]J×KM\in[0,1]^{J\times K} is calculated, where each element mj​km\_{jk} represents the probability that the point xjx\_{j} corresponds to the point yky\_{k}.

A deterministic annealing schedule  is employed to avoid local minima, and the matrix is initialized based on the Euclidean distance of the learned features FF:

|  |  |  |  |
 ---  --- |
|  | mj​k←e−β​(‖Fxj−Fyk‖22−α),m\_{jk}\leftarrow e^{-\beta\left(\|F\_{x\_{j}}-F\_{y\_{k}}\|\_{2}^{2}-\alpha\right)}, |  | (3) | [...] Another important evolution of learning approaches is that they perform correspondence finding in the learned feature space rather than directly on original coordinates. DCP  and PRNet  optimize soft matching matrices, while RPMNet  uses a differentiable Sinkhorn normalization layer  to update a similarity matrix. Alternatively, Predator  and GeoTransformer  predict overlaps between key points to focus on shared regions between two point clouds. Furthermore, some approaches incorporate an outlier filtering scheme to identify and suppress false correspondences. RPMNet  learns an outlier rejection threshold via a parameter estimation network to exclude incorrect matches, while FastMAC  utilizes graph signal processing to prioritize high-frequency nodes and allow for more efficient filtering [...] As illustrated in Fig. 2, R3PM-Net consists of four key components; Feature extraction, correspondence estimation, outlier rejection and transformation parameter estimation. The feature extraction component, the core contribution of R3PM-Net, employs a lightweight network with a global receptive field to process complete input point clouds directly and produce globally-aware features. Next, the correspondence estimation matches the points between the Source (XX) and Target (YY) clouds by employing the extracted features. The outlier rejection module dynamically predicts thresholds to suppress false matches. Finally, a differentiable weighted Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) step  predicts the optimal rigid transformation to align the clouds.

### 3.1 Feature Extraction",True
