### 2.2 Learning Settings and Training Paradigms

Deep learning models for molecular prediction can be developed under various learning settings, depending on data availability and generalization requirements. Let fθf\_{\theta} denote a deep neural network model parameterized by θ\theta. Below we discuss each paradigm in detail. Formal mathematical definitions of the loss functions associated with these paradigms are provided in Appendix A.

#### Supervised Learning. [...] including the toxicity of chemical compounds to organisms or ecosystems, as well as their biodegradability and environmental persistence (119). Across these diverse application domains, the prediction problem is fundamentally framed as establishing structure-property relationships. Given a molecular structure represented in an appropriate format, the task is to predict an associated label or numerical value corresponding to the property of interest. [...] Molecular property prediction (MPP) refers to learning a mapping from a molecular representation to one or more properties or activities of interest (74). Formally, let m∈ℳm\in\mathcal{M} denote an input representation of a molecule (e.g., a graph, sequence, or geometric point cloud) and let y∈𝒴y\in\mathcal{Y} denote the target property. The goal is to learn a function f:ℳ→𝒴f:\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{Y}, parameterized by a deep neural network, that predicts yy from mm with high accuracy. In supervised learning settings, we assume a labeled dataset {(mi,yi)}i=1N\{(m\_{i},y\_{i})\}\_{i=1}^{N} of molecules with known properties, and train ff by minimizing a loss function. Common prediction tasks include binary or multi-class classification(77, 43-equivariant multi-organ toxicity prediction""))",True
