a1_keypoint_matching,A1 — 稀疏对应: 关键点匹配与特征对齐 (Sparse Correspondence: Keypoint Matching),visual correspondences keypoint detection Lie group alignment 2024 2025,8,PAOLI: Pose-free Articulated Object Learning from Sparse-view Images,https://arxiv.org/html/2509.04276v2,arxiv.org,0.07177981,"#### More Qualitative Results

We provide additional qualitative results for correspondences and segmentation in Fig. 13 and Fig. 15, respectively. Besides, correspondences visualization on the real world images are also shown in Fig. 14.

### 6.3 Failure Cases

#### Extreme Initial Misalignment. [...] We provide a qualitative analysis of the correspondence methods in Fig. 4. This figure visualizes the output of the TEASER  solver when fed correspondences from each baseline. Inliers for the two main parts are colored blue and orange, while outliers and non-correspondences are gray. The results visually confirm our quantitative findings. The GaussReg  baseline fails completely; its point cloud is rendered entirely gray, confirming that its 1024 sparse and noisy keypoint correspondences are insufficient to initialize segmentation. FM-Dense  proves highly unstable; the visualization shows most of its 20k correspondences are rejected as outliers, and even when a solution is found (e.g., Laptop), segmentation is incorrect, with inliers (blue/orange) assigned to the same part. In contrast, [...] In summary, our main contributions are:

Problem formulation: We are the first to address articulated object reconstruction from sparse, unposed images, identifying the 3D-to-3D correspondence problem between unaligned reconstructions as the central challenge.

Dense correspondence via deformation: We propose a robust correspondence detector, using a dense deformation field, which is optimized with 3D and 2D losses. This approach outperforms keypoint-based correspondence methods and other adapted baselines.

